<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Paderborn</h1><p class="st-meta">1281 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Paderborn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Westphalian church state rooted in Charlemagne's Saxon wars and his 799 meeting with Pope Leo III, a Catholic bastion until 1802.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> held assemblies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> from 777 and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> there in 799, the prelude to his imperial coronation. The diocese was founded around the same time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meinwerk">Meinwerk</a> in the early 11th century built up its wealth and churches. The bishops gradually acquired comital rights, ruling a territorial principality by the late 13th century, around 1281.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter and estates shared power with the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> spread in the 16th century, but Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dietrich-von-furstenberg">Dietrich von Fürstenberg</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> university in 1614. His relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-furstenberg">Ferdinand von Fürstenberg</a> was a noted scholar-bishop. The territory, in the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellweg">Hellweg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egge-hills">Egge hills</a>, was agricultural with small towns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 the see was secularised and annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> from 1807 to 1813 and then returned to Prussia. The diocese, raised to an archdiocese in 1930, continues.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-paderborn.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Paderborn" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: ZH2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Prince-Bishopric_of_Paderborn_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1281 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paderborn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meinwerk, Dietrich von Fürstenberg, Ferdinand von Fürstenberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Paderborn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1542&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-paderborn" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Paderborn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4878 389 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Paderborn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5198 645L5229 694M5198 645L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5198 645h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5198 645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Paderborn. <a href="/atlas?year=1542&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-paderborn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
